{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Scott Free Productions is a British film and television production company founded by filmmakers and brothers Ridley Scott and Tony Scott.","title":"Scott Free Productions"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" They formed the feature film development company Percy Main Productions in 1980, naming the company after the English village Percy Main, where their father grew up.","title":"Scott Free Productions"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The company was renamed to Scott Free Productions in 1995.","title":"Scott Free Productions"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Scott Free has produced films ranging from the 2000 Hollywood blockbuster "Gladiator" (2000) to "smaller pictures" like "Cracks" (2009).","title":"Scott Free Productions"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Between productions of "White Squall" (1996) and "G.I. Jane" (1997), Ridley Scott reorganized the company.","title":"Scott Free Productions"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Assassin of Youth (1937) is an exploitation film directed by Elmer Clifton.","title":"Assassin of Youth"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is a pre-WWII movie about the supposed ill effects of cannabis.","title":"Assassin of Youth"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The movie is often considered a clone of the much more famous "Reefer Madness" (sharing cast member Dorothy Short).","title":"Assassin of Youth"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The thriller reflects perfectly the antidrug propaganda of its time.","title":"Assassin of Youth"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Nina, the Flower Girl is a lost 1917 silent drama film produced by D. W. Griffith through his Fine Arts Films and distributed by Triangle Film Corporation.","title":"Nina, the Flower Girl"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The film starred Bessie Love, an up-and-coming ingenue actress.","title":"Nina, the Flower Girl"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It also marked the final acting role for Elmer Clifton, who was by then moving on to directing full-time.","title":"Nina, the Flower Girl"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Fighting Caballero is a 1935 American black-and-white Western B-film produced by Weiss Productions Inc. and distributed by Superior Talking Pictures Inc.","title":"Fighting Caballero"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was one of a series of Westerns starring Rex Lease.","title":"Fighting Caballero"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It was produced by Louis Weiss from a screenplay by Elmer Clifton and George M. Merrick, and directed by Clifton.","title":"Fighting Caballero"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Ten Laps to Go (King of the Speedway) is a 1938 American action/drama film directed by Elmer Clifton.","title":"Ten Laps to Go"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The film stars Rex Lease as a champion race car driver, Duncan Renaldo as his rival, and Muriel Evans as the romantic interest.","title":"Ten Laps to Go"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Former silent film star Marie Prevost has a small role in this film, which would prove to be her last; her death from self-inflicted malnutrition and alcoholism occurred less than six months later.","title":"Ten Laps to Go"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Seven Doors to Death (also known as Vanishing Corpses in its American reissue title) is a 1944 American film directed by Elmer Clifton and starring Chick Chandler, June Clyde and George Meeker.","title":"Seven Doors to Death"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The film is a comedy/mystery that was written by Clifton, a prolific independent film director of the era.","title":"Seven Doors to Death"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Elmer Clifton
